  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Starting-hand analysis",
      "items": [
        "The app generates all 1,326 unique two-card starts and simulates the selected number of rounds for each, shuffling a single deck each round and removing the starting cards before dealing. (js/simulation.js:17–35, 80–90, 93–136; README.md:8, 17)",
        "Rates vary between runs. The displayed best hand is the highest observed win rate; no confidence intervals are calculated. (js/app.js:64–70; README.md:77–81)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Rules and model limits",
      "items": [
        "The player hits below the selected threshold for hard and soft hands and does not use the dealer up-card. The dealer stands above its threshold and hits a soft hand at that threshold when the toggle is enabled. (js/rules.js:82–109; README.md:55–67)",
        "Modded mode checks Five-Card Charlie after threshold-based drawing; a qualifying player is checked before the dealer acts. Natural blackjack has no special priority or payout. (js/simulation.js:40–76; js/rules.js:112–114; README.md:68–73)",
        "Splitting, doubling, surrender, insurance, side bets, dealer peeking, multi-deck shoes and payouts are not modeled. (README.md:73–75)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Results and export",
      "items": [
        "Progress yields to the browser between hands, but calculations still use the main thread; 100,000 rounds per hand means 132.6 million rounds and can be slow. (js/app.js:27–57; README.md:79–81)",
        "CSV has grouped averages and summed counts by starting total, followed by detailed per-hand results. Grouped percentages average the per-hand rates after rounding to one decimal. (js/csv.js:18–66; README.md:83–90)",
        "The browser creates and downloads the CSV; the app does not save a server-side report. (js/csv.js:69–79; README.md:20)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Tests and hosting",
      "items": [
        "npm test uses Node’s built-in runner to cover deck combinations, ace handling, thresholds, simulation counts and CSV output. The GitHub workflow does not run the tests. (package.json:1–7; tests/core.test.js:1–71; .github/workflows/jekyll-docker.yml:1–19)",
        "The README says GitHub Pages hosts the static app. The checked-in workflow runs jekyll build --future in Docker for main pushes and pull requests; it has no Pages publish step. (README.md:128–130; .github/workflows/jekyll-docker.yml:1–19)"
      ]
    }
  ]
